---
// Install, as a doc: the ISO first, because a fresh install is the common
// case; the key enrollment the NVIDIA image needs; the switch for a machine
// already on an atomic Fedora; and what the first login and updates are like.
// The home page's #install is the short version and links here for the rest.
import Iso from '../../components/Iso.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { APP_ID, IMAGES, ORG_ID } from '../../data/site';

const DESCRIPTION =
  'Install Pulsar from the weekly ISO, or switch to it from Fedora Silverblue or Kinoite with one command. How to enroll the NVIDIA key, and how updates work.';

const ENROL = 'sudo mokutil --import /etc/pki/pulsar/MOK.der\nsudo systemctl reboot';
const TAKE = `mokutil --test-key /etc/pki/pulsar/MOK.der   # ...is already enrolled\nsudo bootc switch ${IMAGES.nvidia}:latest\nsudo systemctl reboot`;
const CHECK = 'modinfo -F signer nvidia\nnvidia-smi';
const switchTo = (image: string) => `sudo bootc switch \\\n  ${image}:latest\nsudo systemctl reboot`;
const JSONLD = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'Install Pulsar',
    description: DESCRIPTION,
    about: { '@id': APP_ID },
    author: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    publisher: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    url: new URL('/docs/install', Astro.site).href,
  },
];
const MOK = ['Enroll MOK', 'View key 0', 'Continue', 'Yes', 'password', 'reboot'];
const TOC = [
  { id: 'iso', label: 'A fresh install', level: 2 },
  { id: 'secure-boot', label: 'NVIDIA: enroll the key, once', level: 2 },
  { id: 'switch', label: 'Switch from an atomic Fedora', level: 2 },
  { id: 'first-login', label: 'When you log in', level: 2 },
  { id: 'updates', label: 'Updates', level: 2 },
];
---

<Docs
  title="Install"
  fullTitle="Install Pulsar — the weekly ISO, or one bootc switch"
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  path="/docs/install"
  headings={TOC}
  jsonLd={JSONLD}
>
  <h1>Install</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    A new machine takes one download and a USB stick. A machine already on Fedora Silverblue,
    Kinoite or another atomic Fedora takes one command. Either way, nothing compiles on your
    computer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="iso">A fresh install</h2>
  <Iso />
  <p>
    Boot from the stick and pick <strong>Install Pulsar</strong>. The installer asks which disk to
    use; whatever you choose to erase is gone, so back it up first. Then it restarts into your new
    desktop.
  </p>

  <h2 id="secure-boot">NVIDIA: enroll the key, once</h2>
  <p>
    The NVIDIA image’s driver is signed with Pulsar’s key, so Secure&nbsp;Boot can stay on. Your
    firmware has to be told to trust that key once, through a blue screen called MokManager on the
    next&nbsp;boot. The standard image needs none of this.
  </p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Import the certificate</h3>
      <p>
        <code>mokutil</code> asks for a password you retype at the firmware screen on the next boot.
        You use it once, then never again.
      </p>
      <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="enroll" code={ENROL}></arc-code-block>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Answer MokManager</h3>
      <p>The next boot stops in a blue firmware screen. The sequence is:</p>
      <p class="mok">
        {MOK.map((step, index) => (
          <>
            <arc-kbd>{step}</arc-kbd>
            {index < MOK.length - 1 && <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>}
          </>
        ))}
      </p>
      <p class="hint">Miss the prompt and nothing breaks; the enrollment just doesn’t happen. Run the import again.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Switching, not installing? Take the NVIDIA image</h3>
      <p>From the ISO you are already on it. From another image, move over once the key is in:</p>
      <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="switch" code={TAKE}></arc-code-block>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Check the driver loaded</h3>
      <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="check" code={CHECK}></arc-code-block>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <arc-alert variant="warning" heading="Lost the key later?">
    A BIOS update can clear the whole MOK list. On the NVIDIA image the recovery is GNOME
    Software’s own Secure Boot prompt: it enrolls <code>/etc/pki/akmods/certs/public_key.der</code>,
    and a boot-time unit keeps that file equal to <code>MOK.der</code>, so the prompt enrolls the
    right key. If you would rather not trust someone else’s key at all, the standard image needs
    none, and the repo is MIT-licensed to fork with your own.
  </arc-alert>

  <h2 id="switch">Switch from an atomic Fedora</h2>
  <p>
    On Fedora Silverblue, Kinoite or another atomic Fedora 44 or newer, you can move to Pulsar in
    place, with no USB stick. Your files and accounts stay as they are, and the image you came from
    stays in the boot menu until the next update.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="standard" code={switchTo(IMAGES.vanilla)}></arc-code-block>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="nvidia" code={switchTo(IMAGES.nvidia)}></arc-code-block>
  <p class="hint">For the NVIDIA image, <a href="#secure-boot">enroll the key</a> first.</p>

  <h2 id="first-login">When you log in</h2>
  <p>
    A fresh account comes up in Pulsar’s own theme. <code>pulsar theme list</code> shows the rest,{' '}
    <code>pulsar theme set gruvbox</code> switches the whole desktop, and{' '}
    <code>pulsar theme revert</code> gives you stock GNOME. If you switched an account you had
    already made your own, Pulsar leaves it exactly as it was and tells you once that themes are
    there.
  </p>
  <p>
    The apps (Steam, Heroic, a video and a music player, the stock GNOME set and a few more) install
    on the first boot that has a network. A machine you switched onto Pulsar gets anything new with{' '}
    <code>sudo pulsar setup apps</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="updates">Updates</h2>
  <p>
    You are told, and nothing installs itself. A few minutes after you log in, and every six hours
    after that, Pulsar checks whether a newer build is published and sends one desktop notification
    per build.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>sudo pulsar update</code> fetches it, and it takes effect on your next restart. GNOME
    Software’s automatic updates and bootc’s update timer are both off on purpose: the first cannot
    read an image with layered packages, and the second reboots on its&nbsp;own.
  </p>
  <p>
    Layered anything with <code>rpm-ostree install</code>? Use <code>pulsar update</code> rather
    than <code>bootc upgrade</code>. <a href="/docs/cli#update">Why, in the pulsar command</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>

<style>
  .steps {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0 var(--space-xl);
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    counter-reset: step;
  }
  .steps > li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 3rem;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .steps > li::before {
    counter-increment: step;
    content: counter(step);
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    color: var(--accent-primary);
    text-shadow: var(--glow-xs);
    line-height: var(--heading-lh);
  }
  .steps h3 {
    margin-top: 0 !important;
  }
  .mok {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .arrow {
    color: var(--text-ghost);
  }
</style>
